The statement says: "Inside a parent class, the expression new static() always creates an object of the parent class."

In C#, `static` in `new static()` refers to the current class at compile time. If you're inside the parent class (e.g., `Base`), then `new static()` creates an instance of `Base`, not a derived class. This is because `static` is resolved at compile time to the class in which the code is written.

However, there's a nuance: `static` in C# refers to the current class. If you write `new static()` inside a method of the `Base` class, it creates an object of `Base`. It does NOT create an object of the derived class, even if the instance is actually of a derived class. This is different from virtual dispatch.

So the statement is TRUE. Inside the parent class, `new static()` always creates an object of the parent class (the class where the code is written).

